# What is translated of this program's messages, in Ukrainian.
#
# Keyed by the English wording, the same as the German file beside it.
# All of the program's texts stand here since 13.9.2026 -- the captions
# the window carries and the report that runs beside them, Resolve
# included; until then it was the captions alone. Every text that
# is not in this file appears in English: T() gives back the key it was
# handed when it finds no entry, so what is missing reads rather than
# breaks.
#
# These translations were written by Claude, an Anthropic model, out of
# what it knows. No translation service was asked, no Ukrainian speaker
# has read them, and nobody has reviewed a single line. Treat every
# entry as a first draft. Correcting one costs nothing but this file:
# delete an entry and that text is English again.
#
# The 1131 texts and 31 counted things added on 13.9.2026 were written
# by one model, four languages at a time from one sheet. A sample of
# them was read back by another, which found 24 places to put right;
# the same day every one of them was read back in full, against the
# English, the line of code it stands in and the words this catalogue
# used before, and 49 more were put right -- mostly a word the
# catalogue already says otherwise, now and then a meaning that had
# slipped. Treat them as the first draft they still are.
#
# Anybody who speaks Ukrainian is welcome to take part and make this
# better, and one entry is a whole contribution. Change the msgstr,
# leave the msgid exactly as it stands -- it is the key the program
# looks a text up by -- and send it in. Nothing else in the program has
# to be touched, and nothing here is anybody's to defend: the file was
# written to be corrected.
#
# One entry per message: msgid is the English wording the
# program is written in, msgstr what is said instead. An
# entry left out appears in English.
#
# A counted thing is the exception, and Ukrainian is one of the
# languages the machinery for it exists for: English picks between one
# and everything else, Ukrainian between three. Such an entry carries
# msgid_plural and msgstr[0], msgstr[1], msgstr[2] in place of the
# single msgstr, and the header line below says which count wants which
# of them -- [0] where the count ends in 1 but not in 11, [1] where it
# ends in 2, 3 or 4 but not in 12 to 14, [2] everywhere else. So 1, 21
# and 101 take the first, 2 and 24 the second, 5, 11 and 14 the third.
# The wordings are read as a list, so a form left empty costs that form
# and not the entry.
#
# Two of the three are not always different words. After 2, 3 and 4
# Ukrainian puts the noun in the nominative plural, and in the locative
# -- "on %s cameras" -- the plural is one word for every count above
# one, so [1] and [2] are the same there and that is the language, not
# a slip.
#
# Every form carries the same %s the English wording does: the program
# applies the count to whatever comes back, and a form without it stops
# the run on a number nobody tried.
#
# Neither English wording has an ordinary entry of its own beside
# such a group: the singular would be the same msgid twice, and the
# plural is never asked for on its own -- the program asks the group
# by its singular at every count. Where the rule cannot be read at
# all, a counted thing comes out English.
